window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][42]={"university_physics_en:M42-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M42-L1 – Plasma Parameters, Debye Shielding and Quasineutrality<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M42 – Master: Interdisciplinary Physics and Research Frontiers<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: electrostatics; M22: plasmas;\nM36: charged particles. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M41&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate the electron Debye\nlength and explain when a quasineutral bulk description is\nappropriate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A plasma contains mobile positive and negative charge carriers. An\nelectrostatic perturbation redistributes especially the light electrons\nand is spatially screened. <strong>Quasineutral<\/strong> does not mean\nexact charge cancellation in every microscopic cell: on scales much\nlarger than the Debye length, positive and negative bulk charge\ndensities are often close to equal. Near interfaces and sheaths, this\napproximation fails.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For classical, nearly isotropic Maxwellian electrons with number\ndensity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlinearizing their Boltzmann response and Poisson’s equation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{De}=\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_0 k_B T_e/(n_e e^2)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_BT_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis specified in electronvolts, convert that energy into joules before\nsubstituting SI quantities. Ions can contribute to collective screening;\nthe present formula is for the <strong>electron Debye\nlength<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{De}=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\varepsilon_0 k_B T_e}{n_e e^2}},\\qquad \\lambda_{De}\\propto\\sqrt{\\frac{T_e}{n_e}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a <strong>synthetic<\/strong> plasma with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>18<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_e=10^{18}\\,\\mathrm{m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_BT_e=10\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe formula gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>23.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{De}=23.5\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nScreening therefore has a characteristic length, not a sharply\nterminating boundary. Quadrupling temperature at fixed density doubles\nthe Debye length. The figure sketches an exponentially attenuated\npotential in one direction, not a plasma simulation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_065_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Electrostatic screening from a disturbance: normalized potential profile, Debye length and quasineutral bulk.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Electrostatic screening from a\ndisturbance: normalized potential profile, Debye length and quasineutral\nbulk.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original one-dimensional concept diagram of idealized\nshielding.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{De}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat four times the electron density with fixed temperature?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can quasineutrality break down in a plasma sheath?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{De}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>→<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_e\\to9T_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It halves to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>11.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11.75\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Sheaths can contain substantial charge separation and strong\npotential gradients.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The characteristic length triples.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The screening expression assumes a thermal electron response and weak\nperturbations suitable for linearization. Nonequilibrium plasmas, strong\nmagnetization and multiple populations need additional assumptions. The\ngraphic is one-dimensional and schematic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The next lesson combines plasma temperature and density with energy\nconfinement time in fusion concepts. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M42&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/22-611j-introduction-to-plasma-physics-i-fall-2003/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Introduction to Plasma Physics I: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M42-L1 – Plasma Parameters, Debye Shielding and Quasineutrality · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M42-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M42-L2 – Fusion Plasma, Triple Product and Energy Confinement Time<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M42 – Master: Interdisciplinary Physics and Research Frontiers<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M42-L1: Debye shielding; M24: nuclear\nreactions and energy.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute the dimensional fusion\ntriple product and distinguish plasma gain from net power-plant\noutput.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Thermonuclear fusion requires a sufficient reaction rate and an\nenergy balance capable of offsetting losses. Besides temperature and\nfuel density, the <strong>energy confinement time<\/strong> is central:\nin an idealized energy balance it is stored thermal energy divided by\nthe loss power. It is neither the plasma-pulse duration nor the\nresidence time of any single particle.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>A common comparison figure is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">nT_i\\tau_E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith ion number density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nion temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand energy confinement time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne may report\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{keV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin seconds. Any <strong>numerical threshold<\/strong> is not a universal\nphysical constant: it depends on fuel mix, temperature, radiative\nlosses, reaction products and the specific definition of gain.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{P}_3=nT_i\\tau_E,\\qquad \\tau_E=\\frac{W_{\\mathrm{th}}}{P_{\\mathrm{loss}}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For <strong>synthetic<\/strong> inputs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>19<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=8.0\\times10^{19}\\,\\mathrm{m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_i=10\\,\\mathrm{keV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_E=0.40\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe triple product is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>20<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{P}_3=3.2\\times10^{20}\\,\\mathrm{keV\\,s\\,m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDoubling confinement time at fixed density and temperature doubles this\nvalue. Without a reaction and loss model, neither number alone\nestablishes ignition or net electrical power.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_107_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic dependence of the fusion triple product on density and energy confinement time at fixed temperature.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Synthetic dependence of the fusion triple\nproduct on density and energy confinement time at fixed\ntemperature.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic parameter study of the fusion triple product.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The curves are not measured data and do not indicate an ignition\nboundary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{P}_3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_E=0.80\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith other values unchanged.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is energy confinement time not pulse duration?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a higher triple product not directly give power-plant\nefficiency?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.4<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>20<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.4\\times10^{20}\\,\\mathrm{keV\\,s\\,m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is set by stored thermal energy and loss rate; the discharge can\ncontinue much longer with heating.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Heating, fueling, cooling and thermal-to-electric conversion must\nalso be accounted for.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>This is a dimensional scaling illustration, not an ignition-threshold\nclaim. Ion/electron temperatures, transport, impurities, heat losses and\nplasma stability enter full fusion modeling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Another interdisciplinary model class describes flows in a rotating\nreference frame. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M42&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/22-611j-introduction-to-plasma-physics-i-fall-2003/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Introduction to Plasma Physics I: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M42-L2 – Fusion Plasma, Triple Product and Energy Confinement Time · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.eurofusion.org/fusion/fusion-conditions/\">EUROfusion – Fusion Conditions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M42-L2 – Fusion Plasma, Triple Product and Energy Confinement Time · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://euro-fusion.org/glossary/confinement-time/\">EUROfusion – Confinement Time<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M42-L2 – Fusion Plasma, Triple Product and Energy Confinement Time · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M42-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M42-L3 – Geostrophic Balance, Rossby Number and the Beta Effect<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M42 – Master: Interdisciplinary Physics and Research Frontiers<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M38-L2: rotating flows; M14: fluid\ndynamics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute geostrophic velocity\nfrom a horizontal pressure gradient and use Rossby number to assess\nvalidity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Large-scale atmospheric and oceanic currents experience Coriolis\nacceleration in a rotating frame. When acceleration and friction are\nrelatively small, the horizontal pressure-gradient and Coriolis terms\ncan nearly balance. <strong>Geostrophic balance<\/strong> is a\nleading-order approximation rather than the full equations of motion; it\nis associated with small Rossby number away from the equator.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neast,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnorth and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nup, the Northern Hemisphere has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=2\\Omega\\sin\\varphi&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒛<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒖<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\,\\hat{\\mathbf z}\\times\\mathbf u_g=-(1/\\rho)\\nabla_h p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollow\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_g=-(\\rho f)^{-1}\\partial_y p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_g=(\\rho f)^{-1}\\partial_x p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRossby number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Ro}=U/(|f|L)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncompares advective acceleration with Coriolis effects. A local\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-plane\nrepresents\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≃<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(y)\\simeq f_0+\\beta y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_g=-\\frac{1}{\\rho f}\\frac{\\partial p}{\\partial y},\\quad v_g=\\frac{1}{\\rho f}\\frac{\\partial p}{\\partial x},\\quad \\mathrm{Ro}=\\frac{U}{|f|L}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">45^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnorth,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.03<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\approx1.03\\times10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1.2\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand northward pressure increase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.001<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.001\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\,m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe zonal velocity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_g\\approx-8.1\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<strong>westward<\/strong>. Using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=8.1\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1000\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.078<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Ro}\\approx0.078<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure makes the sign convention explicit.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_114_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Northward pressure gradient and westward geostrophic flow in the Northern Hemisphere.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Northward pressure gradient and westward\ngeostrophic flow in the Northern Hemisphere.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original vector diagram with east/north/up axes; not to\nscale.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to the sign of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_y p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nturns negative?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Ro}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=16.2\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith otherwise unchanged values.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the formula fail if applied unchanged at the equator?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The geostrophic zonal flow is then eastward.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.157<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Ro}\\approx0.157<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Near the equator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\to0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nviolating the assumed leading-order Coriolis balance.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The calculation assumes a locally planar, frictionless,\nconstant-density model. Vertical hydrostatics, turbulence, curvature and\nrapidly varying flow add further terms. Small Rossby number alone does\nnot prove exact balance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Applied physics also needs explicit assumptions about resources and\nlife cycles. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M42&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-12-001-topics-in-fluid-dynamics-fall-2024/pages/essay-3-a-coriolis-tutorial/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – A Coriolis Tutorial<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M42-L3 – Geostrophic Balance, Rossby Number and the Beta Effect · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M42-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M42-L4 – Material Efficiency, Life-Cycle Boundaries and Emissions Accounting<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M42 – Master: Interdisciplinary Physics and Research Frontiers<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M35: materials science; M38: climate\nmodels; M42-L3: model boundaries.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate a transparent\nmaterials-substitution scenario and identify the limits of simple\nemission factors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Material efficiency includes durable design, material reduction per\nfunctional unit, reuse, repair and recycling. An environmental\ncomparison needs both a <strong>functional unit<\/strong> and a\n<strong>system boundary<\/strong>: construction, production, use and end\nof life are not automatically covered by one factor. The IEA’s 2019\nreport illustrates strategies, but does not provide timeless emission\nfactors applicable to all projects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a simplified cradle-to-gate comparison, let a functionally\nequivalent part have mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=m_v+m_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn accounting example writes upstream emissions as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=m_v e_v+m_r e_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith consistently defined factors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReal assessments must state recycling allocation, transport,\nmanufacturing yield, functional equivalence and any avoided-burden\nassumptions.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=m_v e_v+m_r e_r,\\qquad r_E=\\frac{E_0-E_1}{E_0}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>A wholly <strong>synthetic<\/strong> comparison uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmaterial. Scenario 0 uses only virgin input with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_v=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,CO_2e/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0=2000\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,CO_2e}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nScenario 1 combines\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>700<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">700\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvirgin and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsecondary input with assumed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_r=0.5\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,CO_2e/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1550<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1=1550\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,CO_2e}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na calculated reduction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>22.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_E=22.5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is <strong>not a real manufacturing inventory<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_163_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Bar chart of synthetic upstream emissions for virgin and secondary material.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Bar chart of synthetic upstream emissions\nfor virgin and secondary material.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original accounting chart with assumed factors.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Not external data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">500\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvirgin and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">500\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsecondary material.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What boundary question must be resolved before calling the\ndifference a “saving”?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should a scenario published in 2019 not be treated as a\nuniversal current material factor?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1250<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=1250\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,CO_2e}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Functional unit, system boundary, recycling allocation and transport\nmust be comparable.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Technology, electricity mix, recycling process and assumptions vary\nacross time and geography.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>These numeric example values do not come from the IEA report. The\nmodel excludes product lifetime, other environmental impacts, costs,\nrebound and actual availability of recycled feedstock.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Searches for unknown phenomena similarly depend on explicit models\nand detection limits. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M42&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.iea.org/reports/material-efficiency-in-clean-energy-transitions\">IEA – Material Efficiency in Clean Energy Transitions (2019)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M42-L4 – Material Efficiency, Life-Cycle Boundaries and Emissions Accounting · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M42-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M42-L5 – Particle Searches, Null Results and Poisson Upper Limits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M42 – Master: Interdisciplinary Physics and Research Frontiers<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M36: particle physics and counting\nexperiments; M30: Standard Model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive a Poisson upper bound\nfrom an idealized zero-count result and distinguish it from evidence for\na discovery.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Searches for axions, dark photons or supersymmetric particles test\nmodels beyond the established particle spectrum. A <strong>null\nresult<\/strong> does not generically disprove an entire theory; it\nconstrains parameter regions subject to a stated signal and detector\nmodel. The Particle Data Group catalogues such searches; listing a\nhypothesis is not evidence of discovery.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal background-free Poisson count with expectation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(N=0\\mid\\mu)=e^{-\\mu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis observed, a one-sided classical upper bound set by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>95<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(N=0\\mid\\mu_{95})=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>95<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>3.00<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{95}=-\\ln(0.05)\\simeq3.00<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith known efficiency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand exposure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe bound on a rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>95<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>95<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{95}=\\mu_{95}/(\\varepsilon\\mathcal E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>95<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.996<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(N=0\\mid\\mu)=e^{-\\mu},\\qquad \\mu_{95}=-\\ln(0.05)\\approx 2.996.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>synthetic<\/strong> search records zero events, assumes no\nbackground and has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon=0.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent exposure units. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>95<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.996<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.99<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{95}\\approx2.996/(0.5\\times10^6)=5.99\\times10^{-6}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nevents per exposure unit. The 95% classical confidence construction has\na repeated-sampling coverage interpretation; it is <strong>not a 95%\nposterior probability<\/strong> that the parameter lies below the\nreported value.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_189_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Poisson probability of zero events versus expected count with the 95% threshold.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Poisson probability of zero events versus\nexpected count with the 95% threshold.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Idealised Poisson zero-count probability with a 95%\nthreshold.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>This is a teaching model, not an actual experimental exclusion\nmeasurement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>90<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{90}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>90<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-\\mu_{90}}=0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>95<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{95}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif efficiency halves with all else unchanged?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a null result not prove that no hypothetical particles\nexist?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>90<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.303<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{90}=-\\ln(0.10)\\approx2.303<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The rate bound doubles when efficiency halves.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The bound applies only within the specified parameter and\nbackground-free detector model.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Actual analyses account for background, statistical and systematic\nuncertainties, signal shapes, detector acceptance and multiple testing.\nThe Poisson zero-count exercise is not a published exclusion curve or a\ndiscovery statistic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Every experimental bound depends on documented and auditable data and\ncomputation. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M42&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/2026/reviews/searches-and-hypothetical-particles.html\">Particle Data Group – Review of Particle Physics 2026: Searches &amp; Hypothetical Particles<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M42-L5 – Particle Searches, Null Results and Poisson Upper Limits · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/programs-projects/nistsematech-engineering-statistics-handbook\">NIST – NIST/SEMATECH Engineering Statistics Handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M42-L5 – Particle Searches, Null Results and Poisson Upper Limits · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M42-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M42-L6 – Good Research Practice, Reproducibility and Uncertainty<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M42 – Master: Interdisciplinary Physics and Research Frontiers<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M31: verification and validation;\nM32: instrumentation; M42-L5: counting statistics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Estimate standard error in a\nsimple independent measurement model and identify essential components\nof reproducible analysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Good research practice extends beyond archiving a final plot:\nmeasurement logs, calibration, selection criteria, analysis-code\nversions, processing steps and justified exclusions must be traceable.\nReproduction may fail because raw data or parameter settings are\nunavailable. The DFG guidelines provide an integrity framework;\nsubject-specific requirements and legal access restrictions still\nmatter. FAIR means <strong>findable, accessible under defined\nconditions, interoperable and reusable<\/strong>, not necessarily\nunrestricted public release.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent identically distributed measurements\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith finite standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe standard deviation of the mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SE}(\\bar X)=\\sigma/\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnknown\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis commonly estimated by sample standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA shared systematic calibration bias <strong>does not<\/strong> shrink\nunder this\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscaling. Correlations may reduce the effective sample size.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>σ<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SE}(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt N},\\qquad \\mathrm{SE}(\\bar X)\\propto N^{-1/2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>synthetic<\/strong> measurement study has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent observations with known spread\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunits. Its standard error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SE}(\\bar X)=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunits. Increasing to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreduces that random component to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.4<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA shared offset of, say,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunit remains unchanged. A reproducible package therefore needs original\ndata, configuration, computation environment, analysis version and a\nverification log.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_245_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Statistical standard error falls with sample size while systematic offset stays constant.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Statistical standard error falls with\nsample size while systematic offset stays constant.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Original model diagram with synthetic parameters; not measured\ndata.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many independent measurements are needed for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SE}=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does a larger sample remove a fixed calibration bias?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What metadata make excluded observations auditable?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=(4/0.2)^2=400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Systematic offset and sampling scatter are distinct uncertainty\ncomponents.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Prespecified criteria, documented rationale, raw records and\nversioned processing steps.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrule should not be used uncritically for correlated, drifting, heavily\nselected or heavy-tailed measurements. Small-sample inference can\nrequire explicit distributional assumptions and intervals. The DFG\nframework does not replace subject-specific data security checks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>M42 links selected research frontiers to evidence standards. M43\nconcludes the pathway with critical model assessment and research\npractice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.dfg.de/en/basics-topics/basics-and-principles-of-funding/good-research-practice/code-of-conduct\">DFG – Code of Conduct: Guidelines for Safeguarding Good Research Practice<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M42-L6 – Good Research Practice, Reproducibility and Uncertainty · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/programs-projects/nistsematech-engineering-statistics-handbook\">NIST – NIST/SEMATECH Engineering Statistics Handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M42-L6 – Good Research Practice, Reproducibility and Uncertainty · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
